      "id": "ff72c035-a99b-4fa5-9ce7-058622c7ffe8",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "Great question — this is **excellent code to learn lambdas, functional interfaces, and anonymous classes**. I’ll walk through it **line by line**, explain *why* each part exists, and then connect it to the **core Java concepts you’re learning**.\n\n---\n\n## 1️⃣ The `Person` record (immutable data holder)\n\n```java\nrecord Person (String firstName, String lastName) {\n    @Override\n    public String toString() {\n        return firstName + \" \" + lastName;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### What is happening?\n\n* `record` is a **special Java class** (Java 16+)\n* Automatically gives you:\n\n  * `private final` fields\n  * constructor\n  * getters → `firstName()` and `lastName()`\n  * `equals`, `hashCode`, `toString` (you override `toString`)\n\n### Why record here?\n\nBecause `Person` is:\n\n* simple\n* immutable\n* data-focused\n\nPerfect for sorting examples.\n\n---\n\n## 2️⃣ Creating the list of people\n\n```java\nList<Person> people = new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(\n        new Person(\"Lucy\", \"Van Pelt\"),\n        new Person(\"Sally\", \"Brown\"),\n        new Person(\"Linus\", \"Van Pelt\"),\n        new Person(\"Peppermint\", \"Patty\"),\n        new Person(\"Charlie\", \"Brown\")));\n```\n\n### What this gives you\n\nAn **unsorted list**:\n\n```\nLucy Van Pelt\nSally Brown\nLinus Van Pelt\nPeppermint Patty\nCharlie Brown\n```\n\nThis list is what you’ll sort using **different techniques**.\n\n---\n\n## 3️⃣ Anonymous class version (old-school Java)\n\n```java\nvar comparatorLastName = new Comparator<Person>() {\n\n    @Override\n    public int compare(Person o1, Person o2) {\n        return o1.lastName().compareTo(o2.lastName());\n    }\n};\n```\n\n### Key ideas here\n\n* `Comparator<Person>` is a **functional interface**\n* You are creating an **anonymous class**\n* You must:\n\n  * write `new Comparator<Person>()`\n  * override `compare()`\n\n### What does `compare()` do?\n\n```java\nreturn o1.lastName().compareTo(o2.lastName());\n```\n\n* compares last names alphabetically\n* returns:\n\n  * negative → `o1` comes first\n  * zero → equal\n  * positive → `o2` comes first\n\n💡 This is **verbose**, but it’s the foundation for lambdas.\n\n---\n\n## 4️⃣ Lambda expression (modern Java)\n\n```java\npeople.sort((o1, o2) -> o1.lastName().compareTo(o2.lastName()));\nSystem.out.println(people);\n```\n\n### Why this works\n\nBecause:\n\n```java\nComparator<Person>\n```\n\nhas **exactly ONE abstract method**:\n\n```java\nint compare(T o1, T o2);\n```\n\nSo Java allows this shortcut:\n\n```\n(o1, o2) -> expression\n```\n\n### What changed?\n\n| Anonymous Class      | Lambda |\n| -------------------- | ------ |\n| `new Comparator<>()` | ❌      |\n| `@Override`          | ❌      |\n| method name          | ❌      |\n| just logic           | ✅      |\n\n### Output after this sort\n\n```\n[Sally Brown, Charlie Brown, Peppermint Patty, Lucy Van Pelt, Linus Van Pelt]\n```\n\n(sorted by **last name only**)\n\n---\n\n## 5️⃣ Local interface inside `main()` (important concept!)\n\n```java\ninterface EnhancedComparator<T> extends Comparator<T> {\n    int secondLevel(T o1, T o2);\n}\n```\n\n### What is this?\n\n* A **local interface**\n* Defined **inside a method**\n* Extends `Comparator<T>`\n\n### Why do this?\n\nYou want:\n\n1. primary sorting (last name)\n2. secondary sorting (first name)\n\n---\n\n## 6️⃣ Anonymous class implementing multiple methods\n\n```java\nvar comparatorMixed = new EnhancedComparator<Person>() {\n```\n\nBecause `EnhancedComparator` has:\n\n* `compare()` (from Comparator)\n* `secondLevel()` (new)\n\n⚠️ **This is no longer a functional interface**\n→ Lambdas **cannot** be used here.\n\n---\n\n## 7️⃣ Multi-level comparison logic\n\n```java\n@Override\npublic int compare(Person o1, Person o2) {\n    int result = o1.lastName().compareTo(o2.lastName());\n    return (result == 0 ? secondLevel(o1, o2) : result);\n}\n```\n\n### What’s happening step-by-step\n\n1. Compare last names\n2. If different → return result\n3. If same → compare first names\n\nEquivalent logic:\n\n```\nsort by lastName\nif lastName same:\n   sort by firstName\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8️⃣ Secondary comparison\n\n```java\n@Override\npublic int secondLevel(Person o1, Person o2) {\n    return o1.firstName().compareTo(o2.firstName());\n}\n```\n\nThis breaks ties when:\n\n* `Van Pelt` vs `Van Pelt`\n* `Brown` vs `Brown`\n\n---\n\n## 9️⃣ Final sort and output\n\n```java\npeople.sort(comparatorMixed);\nSystem.out.println(people);\n```\n\n### Final sorted result\n\n```\n[Charlie Brown, Sally Brown, Peppermint Patty, Linus Van Pelt, Lucy Van Pelt]\n```\n\nNow sorted by:\n1️⃣ Last name\n2️⃣ First name\n\n---\n\n## 🔑 Core Concepts You Learned From This Code\n\n### ✔ Anonymous classes\n\n* Used when more than one method is needed\n\n### ✔ Functional interfaces\n\n* `Comparator` → 1 abstract method → lambdas allowed\n\n### ✔ Lambdas\n\n* Only work with **single abstract method interfaces**\n\n### ✔ Why lambdas fail sometimes\n\nBecause this interface:\n\n```java\nEnhancedComparator\n```\n\nhas **two abstract methods**, so Java doesn’t know which one your lambda is implementing.\n\n---\n\n## 🚀 Modern Java shortcut (FYI)\n\nIn real projects, you’d usually write this instead:\n\n```java\npeople.sort(\n    Comparator.comparing(Person::lastName)\n              .thenComparing(Person::firstName)\n);\n```\n\nThis uses **method references + default methods** — which is exactly where this lesson is heading next.\n\n---\n\nIf you want, next I can:\n\n* rewrite this using **method references**\n* show **why `Comparator` default methods matter**\n* explain **why lambdas work only for functional interfaces**\n\nJust tell me 👍\n",
      "metadata": {
